Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a string stream to hold the HTML output
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the markdown text to the string stream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML string
    return htmlStream.str();
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. The `std::stringstream` class is used to write the markdown text to a string stream, which is then converted to a `std::string` and returned.

Here's an example usage:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a markdown text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p>This is a markdown text.</p>
```

Note that this function does not perform any parsing or conversion of the markdown text. It simply writes the markdown text to a string stream and returns the result.

